        heading: "What economics is really about",
        html: `<p><strong>Economics</strong> is the study of how people, businesses and governments make choices about using <em>limited</em> resources to satisfy <em>unlimited</em> wants. That gap between what we want and what we have is called <strong>scarcity</strong>, and it is the starting point for every economic question. Scarcity does not mean something is rare. Even a rich country has scarce time, workers and money, because there is never enough to do everything at once.</p>
<p>Because resources are scarce, every choice has a <strong>trade-off</strong> (picking one option means passing on others) and an <strong>opportunity cost</strong>: the value of the <em>single next-best</em> alternative you gave up. Every society must answer three basic questions: <em>what</em> to produce, <em>how</em> to produce it and <em>for whom</em>.</p>
<p><strong>Economic activities</strong> are the three things an economy does with resources:</p>
<ul><li><strong>Production</strong>: making goods and services (a bakery baking bread).</li><li><strong>Distribution</strong>: getting them to the people who want them (trucks, stores, apps).</li><li><strong>Consumption</strong>: using them up to satisfy wants (you eating the bread).</li></ul>
<p>Economists also split the field in two. <strong>Microeconomics</strong> looks at individual households, firms and markets (why did the price of eggs rise?). <strong>Macroeconomics</strong> looks at the whole economy (inflation, unemployment, GDP).</p>
<div class="example"><h4>Example</h4><p>Jordan has a free Saturday. He can work a shift for $90, study for a finance test, or go to a concert. He chooses the concert. If working was his next-best choice, his opportunity cost is the $90 he could have earned, not the total of every option he skipped.</p></div>
<div class="tip"><p>Opportunity cost is always <strong>one</strong> alternative: the next-best one. Exam distractors often add up all the options you skipped.</p></div>`
