<ol><li><strong>Signal (information)</strong>: prices tell buyers and sellers what is scarce. A rising price signals "people want more of this than is available."</li>
<li><strong>Incentive</strong>: prices motivate behavior. A higher price encourages producers to make more and encourages consumers to buy less or look for substitutes. A lower price does the reverse.</li>
<li><strong>Rationing (allocation)</strong>: prices divide up scarce goods among the people most willing and able to pay. Concert tickets go to buyers who value them most, instead of by lottery or waiting in line.</li></ol>
