      {
        pi: "EC:104",
        heading: "The global environment businesses operate in",
        html: `<p>Even a business that never ships a product overseas operates in a <strong>global environment</strong>. Its suppliers, competitors, customers, lenders and even its prices are tied to events in other countries.</p>
<ul><li><strong>Global supply chains</strong>: a smartphone may be designed in one country, use chips from another and be assembled in a third. A port closure or shortage anywhere can delay products everywhere.</li>
<li><strong>Global competition</strong>: domestic firms compete with foreign firms selling online or through imports.</li>
<li><strong>Global customers</strong>: the internet lets even small firms sell abroad.</li>
<li><strong>Global financial markets</strong>: money moves across borders in seconds. Interest rates, stock prices and exchange rates in one country affect others.</li>
<li><strong>International institutions and rules</strong>: the World Trade Organization (WTO) sets trade rules, the International Monetary Fund (IMF) works on monetary stability and lends to countries in crisis, and the World Bank funds development projects.</li></ul>
<p>Firms enter global markets in steps of increasing commitment and risk: <strong>exporting</strong> (selling abroad), <strong>licensing</strong> or <strong>franchising</strong> (letting a foreign firm use your brand or technology for a fee), forming a <strong>joint venture</strong> with a foreign partner, and finally <strong>foreign direct investment</strong> (owning facilities abroad). Companies operating in many countries are called <strong>multinational corporations</strong>.</p>
<p>Operating globally adds new risks: <strong>currency (exchange-rate) risk</strong>, <strong>political risk</strong>, different laws and cultures, and longer, more fragile supply chains.</p>
<div class="example"><h4>Example</h4><p>A U.S. bank that finances a client's imports from Japan must think about the yen-dollar exchange rate, Japanese and U.S. trade rules, and the risk that the shipment is delayed. That is the global environment at work.</p></div>`
      },
      {
        pi: "EC:105",
        heading: "Factors that affect the business environment",
        html: `<p>The <strong>business environment</strong> is everything outside and inside a company that affects how it operates. The <strong>external environment</strong> is usually beyond the company's control, so businesses scan it constantly. A handy framework is <strong>PESTLE</strong>:</p>
<table><thead><tr><th>Factor</th><th>Examples</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td><strong>Political</strong></td><td>Government stability, trade policy, tax policy, elections</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Economic</strong></td><td>Interest rates, inflation, unemployment, the business cycle, exchange rates</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Social</strong></td><td>Demographics (an aging population), lifestyles, values, education levels</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Technological</strong></td><td>Artificial intelligence, mobile payments, automation, cybersecurity threats</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Legal</strong></td><td>Consumer protection laws, employment laws, financial regulation</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Environmental</strong></td><td>Climate, natural disasters, sustainability expectations</td></tr>
</tbody></table>
<p>Competition is also a major external force. The <strong>internal environment</strong> includes things the business <em>can</em> control: its employees, culture, finances, technology and management. A <strong>SWOT analysis</strong> combines both views: strengths and weaknesses are internal; opportunities and threats are external.</p>
<p>DECA exams like to ask you to <strong>classify</strong> a change. Practice: a new law capping credit card late fees is <em>legal/political</em>; more customers wanting to bank by phone is <em>social and technological</em>; rising interest rates are <em>economic</em>.</p>
<div class="example"><h4>Example</h4><p>A credit union notices more members are retiring (social), rates have climbed (economic) and rivals offer AI chat support (technological). It responds by expanding retirement-planning services and adding a digital assistant.</p></div>`
      },
      {
        pi: "EC:106",
        heading: "The nature of business ethics",
        html: `<p><strong>Ethics</strong> are the moral principles that guide what is right and wrong. <strong>Business ethics</strong> apply those principles to decisions at work. Being <em>legal</em> and being <em>ethical</em> are not the same: laws set the minimum, while ethics often ask more. Something can be legal but still unethical, like using confusing fine print to hide fees.</p>
<p>Common ethical issues in business include:</p>
<ul><li><strong>Honesty</strong> in advertising, sales and financial reports.</li>
<li><strong>Conflicts of interest</strong>: when personal gain could bias a decision, such as an advisor pushing a product because it pays a bigger commission.</li>
<li><strong>Confidentiality and privacy</strong> of customer data.</li>
<li><strong>Fair treatment</strong> of employees, customers and suppliers, with no discrimination.</li>
<li><strong>Insider trading</strong>: trading securities on important nonpublic information, which is illegal.</li>
<li><strong>Bribery</strong> and other corrupt payments.</li></ul>
<p>Businesses encourage ethical behavior with a written <strong>code of ethics</strong> (or code of conduct), training, leaders who model good behavior, and safe ways to report problems. <strong>Whistleblowers</strong> who report wrongdoing are protected by several laws.</p>
<p>Why it matters in finance: the industry runs on <strong>trust</strong>. Scandals such as Enron's accounting fraud led to the Sarbanes-Oxley Act (2002), which requires top executives to certify their company's financial statements. Unethical behavior can bring fines, lawsuits, lost customers and a ruined reputation.</p>
<div class="example"><h4>Example</h4><p>Ava, a bank employee, is told to open extra accounts for customers without asking them so the branch hits its sales goal. Even if a manager tells her to, this is unethical (and in fact illegal). The ethical choice is to refuse and report it.</p></div>
<div class="tip"><p>A quick test: would you be comfortable if this decision were on the front page of the news?</p></div>`
      },
      {
        pi: "EC:107",
        heading: "How organizations adapt to today's markets",
        html: `<p>Markets now change faster than ever, so successful organizations treat <strong>adaptability</strong> as a core skill. Ways businesses adapt include:</p>
<ul><li><strong>Embracing technology</strong>: digital channels, automation, data analytics and AI tools.</li>
<li><strong>Customer focus</strong>: using data and feedback to personalize products and service.</li>
<li><strong>Agile methods</strong>: small teams test ideas quickly, learn from customers and improve in short cycles instead of following one long, rigid plan.</li>
<li><strong>Flexible work</strong>: remote and hybrid work, freelancers and gig workers.</li>
<li><strong>Flatter structures</strong>: fewer layers so decisions happen faster.</li>
<li><strong>Partnerships and outsourcing</strong>: working with specialists instead of doing everything in-house.</li>
<li><strong>Diversifying</strong> products, markets and suppliers to spread risk and build resilient supply chains.</li>
<li><strong>Sustainability</strong>: responding to customers and investors who care about environmental impact.</li>
<li><strong>Continuous learning</strong>: training employees for new skills.</li></ul>
<p>Firms that fail to adapt can disappear. A classic example is the video-rental chain that was overtaken by streaming services, or the camera-film company that invented digital photography but was slow to embrace it.</p>
<p><strong>Finance tie-in:</strong> banks have adapted by moving from branch-centered service to mobile apps, mobile check deposit, instant peer-to-peer payments, and partnerships with fintech firms. Many have reduced their number of branches while redesigning remaining ones as advice centers.</p>
<div class="example"><h4>Example</h4><p>A small insurance agency sees younger customers shopping online. It launches online quotes, a texting option and short how-to videos, keeping its personal service while meeting customers where they are.</p></div>`
      }
