        heading: "Cultural considerations in global business relations",
        html: `<p><strong>Culture</strong> is the shared values, beliefs, customs, language and behaviors of a group. When businesspeople from different cultures work together, misunderstandings can cost deals. Key areas to understand:</p>
<ul><li><strong>Language</strong>: translation errors and slogans that mean something unintended in another language.</li>
<li><strong>Nonverbal communication</strong>: gestures, eye contact, personal space and handshakes vary. A thumbs-up or an "OK" sign is friendly in some places and rude in others.</li>
<li><strong>Time</strong>: in some cultures punctuality and strict schedules are expected; in others, time is more flexible and relationships come first.</li>
<li><strong>High-context vs. low-context communication</strong>: in high-context cultures (many East Asian, Middle Eastern and Latin American countries), meaning is carried by relationships, tone and what is <em>not</em> said. In low-context cultures (U.S., Germany), people expect direct, explicit words and detailed written contracts.</li>
<li><strong>Individualism vs. collectivism</strong>: some cultures stress personal achievement; others stress the group, family and harmony, so decisions may take longer and involve more people.</li>
<li><strong>Formality and hierarchy</strong>: use of titles, who speaks first, respect for seniority.</li>
<li><strong>Gift-giving, dining, religion and holidays</strong>: what is polite, and when business pauses.</li></ul>
<p>Two attitudes to know: <strong>ethnocentrism</strong> is judging other cultures by your own culture's standards, which damages relationships. <strong>Cultural sensitivity</strong> (cultural intelligence) means learning and respecting other customs and adapting your behavior.</p>
<div class="example"><h4>Example</h4><p>Ethan, a U.S. banker, pushes to sign a deal in the first meeting with a Japanese client. The client politely says "we will consider it," which in that high-context setting may signal hesitation. Building the relationship first would have worked better.</p></div>
<div class="tip"><p>Research before you go: greetings, business cards, dress, gifts and how decisions are made.</p></div>`
